  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Military radar can make your router switch Wi-Fi channels - here's why",
      "link": "https://www.engadget.com/2307712/military-radar-makes-your-router-change-wifi-channels/",
      "description": "Annoying as it may be, there's a good reason why internet routers around the world give the right of way to certain types of radar.",
      "source_name": "Engadget",
      "published": "2026-11-09T14:15:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "RadarDINO: Distilling a Visual Foundation Model into mmWave Radar for Human Mesh Reconstruction",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09524",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.09524v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Millimeter-wave (mmWave) radar offers a privacy-preserving and illumination-robust alternative for human mesh reconstruction (HMR). However, high-fidelity radar HMR remains challenging. Sparse point cloud-based methods suffer from information loss and missed detections, while raw radar tensor methods like RT-Mesh lack fine-grained semantics and explicit local body modeling. In this paper, we propose RadarDINO, a radar HMR framework that distills visual semantics from a DINOv3 teacher into 3D radar representations and introduces explicit geometric supervision. DINOv3 descriptors are transferred to radar voxels via calibrated RGB-radar geometry, providing voxel- and body-part-level supervision for part-aware radar representations. We further incorporate vertex- and joint-losses to directly constrain reconstructed geometry. On the M4Human dataset, our method outperforms the SOTA baseline across all three evaluation splits.",
      "source_name": "arXiv Computer Vision",
      "published": "2026-11-09T05:00:00"
    }
  ]
}